---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import TimestampConverter from '../../components/TimestampConverter.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Unix Timestamp Converter" description="Convert between Unix timestamps and human-readable dates, both directions, instantly.">
  <TimestampConverter client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>What a Unix timestamp is</h2>
  <p>A Unix timestamp is the number of seconds (or milliseconds) since January 1, 1970 UTC — commonly called "the epoch." It's widely used in APIs, databases, and logs because it's timezone-independent and trivially easy to compare and sort.</p>

  <h2>Features</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Converts a timestamp to a human-readable UTC date</li>
    <li>Automatically detects seconds vs. milliseconds based on digit count</li>
    <li>Instant, client-side conversion</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to use this tool</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Paste a Unix timestamp (in seconds or milliseconds) into the input box.</li>
    <li>The equivalent UTC date and time appears instantly.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>The timestamp <code>1735689600</code> converts to <code>Wed, 01 Jan 2025 00:00:00 GMT</code>.</p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mixing up seconds and milliseconds.</strong> JavaScript's <code>Date</code> object expects milliseconds, while most Unix APIs return seconds — a 1000x error is a common source of "wrong date" bugs.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting timezone context.</strong> A Unix timestamp itself has no timezone — it represents a single instant. The "date" you see depends on which timezone you convert it to for display.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Debugging why a "createdAt" field in an API response looks like a large number</li>
    <li>Converting log timestamps to readable dates during incident investigation</li>
    <li>Checking expiry timestamps in JWTs or session tokens</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Why does my timestamp look wrong by exactly 1000x?</strong> You likely have a seconds-vs-milliseconds mismatch — check whether your source API documents its timestamp unit.</p>
  <p><strong>Does this account for my local timezone?</strong> The primary output is UTC, which is unambiguous; convert to local time as a secondary step if needed for display.</p>
</ToolLayout>
